<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Shaykh al-Mufid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 948–1022</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Shaykh al-Mufid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Shaykh al-Mufid was a Twelver Shia theologian and jurist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and the leading Shia scholar of the Buyid period. He defended Shia doctrine in debates with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Mu'tazili opponents, adopting rational methods in theology, and his Kitab al-Irshad on the lives of the twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imams</a> remains a standard work. His students included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sharif-al-murtada">al-Sharif al-Murtada</a> and al-Shaykh al-Tusi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man was born in 'Ukbara, a small town north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in 336 AH according to one account and 338 AH according to Shaykh Tusi. His family belonged to the al-Harithi clan of the tribe of Madhhaj, and because his father was a teacher he was also called Ibn al-Mu'allim, son of the teacher. He moved with his father to Baghdad, where the Shia Buwayhids were ruling. There he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> with Al-Shaykh al-Saduq and also learned from Ibn Qulawayh and from the Mutazilite scholars Abu Abdallah al-Basri and Ali ibn Isa al-Rummani.</p><p>Tradition explains his title in two ways. One story says al-Rummani gave him the name al-Mufid, the Instructor, after a dispute over the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, Talha and Zubayr against Ali; Ibn Shahr Ashub held that the twelfth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>, Muhammad al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, gave it to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ibn al-Nadim, who knew him, called him the head of the Shia theologians, and al-Tawhidi described him as eloquent and skilful at dialectic. He defended the role of reason in theology. Macdermott places his theology closer to the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> school of Mutazilism than to the Basran system of his contemporary 'Abd al-Jabbar, though he parted from Mutazilism on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> and on grave sin. He held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imams</a> were superior to every prophet but Muhammad and could take the prophets' place in judging, enforcing the law and teaching mankind.</p><p>He disagreed at times with his teacher al-Saduq: his Tashih al-Itiqadat corrects al-Saduq's creed, and he rejected reliance on single traditions for legal rulings. Of some 200 works only about ten survive, among them the Amali, Awail al-Maqalat and al-Muqni'ah, whose commentary by Shaykh Tusi became one of the Shia Four Books.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His library and school were destroyed in attacks during his life. He died on the third day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> in 413 AH; Shaykh Tusi wrote that his funeral drew the largest crowd ever seen. After two years buried in his own house, his body was moved to the Kadhimiya shrine and laid beside Ibn Qulawayh, near the graves of the Imams Musa al-Kadhim and Muhammad al-Jawad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s official calendar marks the ninth day of Azar in his memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Shaykh al-Mufid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under their rule Twelver Shia scholarship flourished in Baghdad, with <b>al-Shaykh al-Mufid</b>, al-Sharif al-Murtada and al-Sharif al-Radi, compiler of the Nahj al-Balagha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=996" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of al-Shaykh al-Mufid"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=996">What was here in 996 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
